        "name": "What is a Registered Dental Hygienist (RDH)?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A Registered Dental Hygienist (RDH) is a licensed preventive oral health professional who specializes in teeth cleaning (scaling and root planing), oral tissue assessments, radiography, and educating patients on customized home care routines."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How often should I have a professional cleaning?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "While the standard is every 6 months, intervals vary depending on individual oral health. Patients with active gingivitis, deep gum pockets, or a history of gum disease (periodontal maintenance) require professional scaling every 3 to 4 months to prevent disease recurrence."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Are electric toothbrushes better than manual ones?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes, clinical research consistently shows that high-quality electric toothbrushes remove up to 21% more plaque and reduce gingivitis by 11% compared to manual toothbrushes, thanks to their oscillation/rotation or sonic technology and built-in timers."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why do my gums bleed when I floss?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Gums bleed because of inflammation (gingivitis), which is caused by plaque bacteria left along the gumline. While it is tempting to stop flossing when they bleed, the solution is to floss daily and gently. The bleeding and inflammation will usually resolve in 7 to 10 days."
        }
      }
